# Project Veronica

[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Python 3.11+](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.11+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)
[![Raspberry Pi 5](https://img.shields.io/badge/Platform-Raspberry%20Pi%205-red.svg)]()

Project Veronica is a distributed classroom capture and edge-control system designed for resource-constrained schools. A Raspberry Pi 5 edge hub orchestrates the camera, microphone, recording, reporting, Telegram, Google Drive, and web interfaces, while a behind-screen ESP32/NFC module provides dependable classroom controls and teacher authentication.

The system is designed for unreliable networks, limited infrastructure, and situations where a physical control surface remains usable when a browser or network is unavailable.

---

## Overview

A typical deployment provides:

- NFC teacher login and subject context switching
- A detachable, height-adjustable, tilt-enabled 800×480 wall display
- Behind-screen PN532 NFC and piezo-buzzer hardware
- A NodeMCU ESP-32S 38-pin bridge communicating with the Pi over TCP
- Chalkboard photo capture with 5-frame noise reduction
- Live camera preview for dashboard, kiosk, and projector views
- Video-and-audio and audio-only lecture recording
- Multi-page PDF lecture-note compilation
- Telegram photos, PDFs, audio, notifications, reports, and an interactive student archive
- Google Drive archival with offline upload spooling
- A Flask dashboard, projector view, principal ledger, kiosk UI, and diagnostic admin portal
- Secure physical shutdown/restart confirmation
- Dynamic runtime configuration through `config.json`
- Hardware-independent preview and demonstration mode

