## Quick start

### 1. Install the Pi dependencies

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
sudo apt install -y ffmpeg fswebcam v4l2-utils alsa-utils chromium
```

### 2. Configure the deployment

Create or edit `config.json`, or update values through `/admin`:

- Wi-Fi credentials
- Pod host and TCP port
- Telegram bot token and chat ID
- Google Drive folder ID
- Google OAuth or service credentials
- Camera and microphone devices
- Subject and NFC mappings
- Inactivity timeout
- Administrator credentials
- Useful-site bookmarks

### 3. Prepare the ESP32 pod

Flash the firmware under `esp32_firmware/esp32_sensor_pod/` for the behind-screen wall pod, or use the legacy touch or keypad profiles when testing those hardware variants.

For the historical 3.5-inch TFT setup, use `esp32_firmware/esp32_touch_pod/esp32_touch_pod.ino` and follow `docs/ESP32_DISPLAY_SETUP_GUIDE.md`.

### 4. Launch Veronica

```bash
python3 main.py
```

Use `--no-kiosk` or `--headless` to prevent automatic Chromium kiosk startup.

### 5. Open the interfaces

```text
http://<pi-ip>:5000/
http://<pi-ip>:5000/pi
http://<pi-ip>:5000/pod
http://<pi-ip>:5000/projector
http://<pi-ip>:5000/report
http://<pi-ip>:5000/login
http://<pi-ip>:5000/admin
```

### 6. Run hardware-free preview mode

```bash
python3 preview_server.py
```

The preview server uses simulated state and simulated hardware responses. It starts on port `5000` and falls back to port `8080` if that port is busy.

